/* NEET past-year questions · Zoology · Evolution. Generated by tools/pyq-ingest/neet-assemble.js: add new papers with that tool, do not edit by hand. */
window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_evolution=[
{"id":"neet-2026-q140","pn":140,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Human evolution","q":"Evolution of human appears parallel to the progressive development of brain and language skills. As such, the evolution of individual species in the sequence of their appearance is:","o":["Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Ramapithecus → Neanderthal → Homo sapiens","Ramapithecus → Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Neanderthal → Homo sapiens","Homo sapiens → Ramapithecus → Homo habilis → Neanderthal → Homo erectus","Neanderthal → Ramapithecus → Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Homo sapiens"],"c":1,"s":"Human evolution appears parallel to the progressive development of brain and language skills.\nThe correct chronological order is Ramapithecus → Australopithecines → Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Neanderthalensis → Homo sapiens.\nSo option (2) is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q149","pn":149,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Homologous organs","q":"Given below are two statements : one is labelled as Assertion A and the other is labelled as Reason R.\nAssertion A : Forelimbs of human and bats are homologous.\nReason R : Forelimbs of humans and bats have similar anatomical structure.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below.","o":["Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A","A is true but R is false","A is false but R is true","Both A and R are correct and R is the correct explanation of A"],"c":3,"s":"Forelimbs of bats and humans have a similar pattern of bone arrangement.\nThey do different functions but have similar anatomical structure: humerus, radius, ulna, carpals, metacarpals and phalanges.\nThe same structure developed in different directions due to adaptation to different needs.\nThis is divergent evolution, so these structures are homologous."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q165","pn":165,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Hardy-Weinberg principle","q":"A population of diploid organisms is at Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium. If the frequency of allele A is 0.1, the frequency of AA is ________.","o":["0.02","0.10","0.99","0.01"],"c":3,"s":"Frequency of allele A, p = 0.1.\nAt Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium, frequency of AA = p².\np² = (0.1)² = 0.01."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q166","pn":166,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Adaptive radiation","q":"Adaptive radiation in placental mammals and Australian Marsupials leading to similarity between distant species is an example of ________.","o":["convergent evolution","founder effect","genetic drift","divergent evolution"],"c":0,"s":"When more than one adaptive radiation appears in an isolated geographical area (representing different habitats), it is called convergent evolution.\nPlacental mammals in Australia also show adaptive radiation into varieties.\nEach of them looks similar to a corresponding marsupial."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q170","pn":170,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Natural selection","q":"Natural selection can lead to ________.\n(a) stabilisation\n(b) genetic drift\n(c) directional change\n(d) disruption\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["(a), (c) and (d) only","(a), (b), (c) and (d)","(a) and (c) only","(a) only"],"c":0,"s":"Natural selection can lead to stabilisation, directional change or disruption.\nIn natural selection, heritable variations that help better survival let organisms reproduce and leave more progeny.\nVariation from mutation, recombination, gene flow or genetic drift changes the frequency of genes and alleles in the next generation."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q174","pn":174,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Evidences for evolution","q":"Which of the following is not evidence for evolution?","o":["Paleontological evidence from fossil records","Embryological support for evolution as proposed by Ernst Heckel","Divergent evolution of anatomical structures such as forelimbs","Convergent evolution of traits like wings of birds and butterflies"],"a":"2/4*","s":"Divergent evolution of structures such as forelimbs shows homology.\nHomology shows common ancestry and is evidence for evolution.\nWhen the same structure develops in different directions for different needs, the structures are homologous.\nConvergent evolution is not evidence, because species can develop similar features without a common ancestor.\nFossils are remains of hard parts of life forms found in rocks.\nStudying fossils in different sedimentary layers shows the geological period in which they lived.\nEmbryological support was proposed by Ernst Heckel from features common to vertebrate embryos but absent in adults.\nLater this proposal was disproved by Karl Ernst von Baer.\nNote: Printed answer is '2/4*' (two options, 2 or 4, with an asterisk); solution supports both: Heckel's proposal was disapproved by von Baer, and convergent evolution is not evidence of common ancestry."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q174","pn":174,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Convergent evolution","q":"Which of the following is not an example of convergent evolution?","o":["Eyes of octopuses and mammals","Fore limbs of whales and bats","Wings of butterflies and birds","Flippers of penguins and dolphins"],"c":1,"s":"Fore limbs of whales and bats are examples of divergent evolution and show homology."},
{"id":"neet-2026r-q176","pn":176,"y":"Re-NEET 2026 · 21 Jun","tp":"Evolution","st":"Origin of man","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: Modern Homo sapiens arose in Australia and moved across continents.\nStatement II: Homo sapiens arose around 75000 to 10000 years ago.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below:","o":["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],"c":2,"s":"Homo sapiens arose in Africa and moved across continents, developing into distinct races. So Statement I is incorrect.\nModern Homo sapiens arose during the ice age, between 75,000 and 10,000 years ago. So Statement II is correct."},
{"id":"neet-2026-q179","pn":179,"y":"NEET 2026 · 3 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"History of life on earth","q":"Match List I with List II with respect to chronology of evolution of life forms\nA. About 65 mya — I. Jawless fish probably evolved\nB. About 500 mya — II. The dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth\nC. About 350 mya — III. Seaweeds and few plants probably existed\nD. About 320 mya — IV. Invertebrates were formed and became active","o":["A(II), B(IV), C(I), D(III)","A(II), B(IV), C(III), D(I)","A(I), B(II), C(III), D(IV)","A(III), B(IV), C(I), D(II)"],"c":0,"s":"About 65 mya: the dinosaurs suddenly disappeared from the earth (A-II).\nAbout 500 mya: invertebrates were formed and became active (B-IV).\nAbout 350 mya: jawless fish probably evolved (C-I).\nAbout 320 mya: seaweeds and few plants probably existed (D-III).\nSo A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q096","pn":96,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Evolution","st":"RNA world, origin of DNA","q":"Given below are two statements :\nStatement I : In the RNA world, RNA is considered the first genetic material evolved to carry out essential life processes. RNA acts as a genetic material and also as a catalyst for some important biochemical reactions in living systems. Being reactive, RNA is unstable.\nStatement II : DNA evolved from RNA and is a more stable genetic material. Its double helical strands being complementary, resist changes by evolving repairing mechanism.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :","o":["Both statement I and statement II are correct","Both statement I and statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but statement II is correct"],"c":0,"s":"In the RNA world, RNA was the first genetic material.\nThere is enough evidence that essential life processes (metabolism, translation, splicing, etc.) evolved around RNA.\nRNA acted as genetic material and also as a catalyst; some biochemical reactions are catalysed by RNA catalysts, not by protein enzymes. So statement I is correct.\nStatement II is also correct: DNA is double stranded with complementary strands, and it resists changes by evolving a repair process."},
{"id":"neet-2025-q151","pn":151,"y":"NEET 2025","tp":"Evolution","st":"Homology and analogy","q":"Sweet potato and potato represent a certain type of evolution. Select the correct combination of terms to explain the evolution.","o":["Analogy, convergent","Homology, divergent","Homology, convergent","Analogy, divergent"],"c":0,"s":"Sweet potato is a root modification while potato is a stem modification, but both have the same function.\nAnalogous structures are not anatomically similar but perform similar functions.\nAnalogous structures are the result of convergent evolution.\nHomologous organs are anatomically similar but do not perform similar functions.\nHomologous organs are the result of divergent evolution."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q162","pn":162,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Convergent evolution","q":"The flippers of the Penguins and Dolphins are the example of the","o":["Convergent evolution","Divergent evolution","Adaptive radiation","Natural selection"],"c":0,"s":"Option (1) is correct.\nThe flippers do the same job but the structures are not anatomically alike, so they are analogous structures.\nDivergent evolution gives homologous structures, so (2) is wrong.\nAdaptive radiation is evolution of different species from one point radiating to other areas, so (3) is wrong.\nNatural selection is a mechanism of evolution, so (4) is wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q164","pn":164,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Hardy-Weinberg principle","q":"Which one of the following factors will not affect the Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium?","o":["Gene migration","Constant gene pool","Genetic recombination","Genetic drift"],"c":1,"s":"Option (2) is correct.\nA constant gene pool does not disturb Hardy-Weinberg equilibrium.\nGene migration, genetic recombination and genetic drift disturb it and lead to evolution."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q165","pn":165,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Human evolution","q":"Given below are some stages of human evolution. Arrange them in correct sequence. (Past to Recent)\nA. Homo habilis\nB. Homo sapiens\nC. Homo neanderthalensis\nD. Homo erectus\nChoose the correct sequence of human evolution from the options given below :","o":["C-B-D-A","A-D-C-B","D-A-C-B","B-A-D-C"],"c":1,"s":"Option (2) is correct.\nFrom past to recent: Homo habilis → Homo erectus → Homo neanderthalensis → Homo sapiens."},
{"id":"neet-2024-q187","pn":187,"y":"NEET 2024 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Geological time scale","q":"Match List I with List II:\nA. Mesozoic Era — I. Lower invertebrates\nB. Proterozoic Era — II. Fish & Amphibia\nC. Cenozoic Era — III. Birds & Reptiles\nD. Paleozoic Era — IV. Mammals\nChoose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["A-I, B-II, C-IV, D-III","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-II, B-I, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-II, D-IV"],"c":1,"s":"Mesozoic Era – III. Birds & Reptiles.\nProterozoic Era – I. Lower invertebrates.\nCenozoic Era – IV. Mammals.\nPaleozoic Era – II. Fish & Amphibia.\nThis gives A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q154","pn":154,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Adaptive radiation","q":"Select the correct group/set of Australian Marsupials exhibiting adaptive radiation.","o":["Lemur, Anteater, Wolf","Tasmanian wolf, Bobcat, Marsupial mole","Numbat, Spotted cuscus, Flying Phalanger","Mole, Flying Squirrel, Tasmanian tiger cat."],"c":2,"s":"Numbat, spotted cuscus and flying phalanger are all Australian marsupials.\nMole and flying squirrel are placental mammals, so (4) is wrong.\nLemur and wolf are placental, so (1) is wrong.\nBobcat is placental, so (2) is wrong."},
{"id":"neet-2023-q176","pn":176,"y":"NEET 2023 · 7 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"RNA genome mutation","q":"Given below are two statements:\nStatement I: RNA mutates at a faster rate.\nStatement II: Viruses having RNA genome and shorter life span mutate and evolve faster.\nIn the light of the above statements, choose the correct answer from the options given below:","o":["Statement I is false but Statement II is true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are true.","Both Statement I and Statement II are false.","Statement I is true but Statement II is false."],"c":1,"s":"RNA is unstable, so it mutates faster.\nViruses with an RNA genome and a short life span therefore mutate and evolve faster.\nBoth statements are true."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q157","pn":157,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Evolution","st":"Natural selection types","q":"Natural selection where more individuals acquire specific character value other than the mean character value, leads to","o":["Directional change","Disruptive change","Random change","Stabilising change"],"c":0,"s":"In directional selection more individuals take a value other than the mean, so the curve shifts one way.\nIn disruptive selection more individuals take values at both ends of the curve.\nNatural selection has no random change.\nStabilisation happens when more individuals take the mean value."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q165","pn":165,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Origin of life theories","q":"Panspermia, an idea that is still a favourite for some astronomers, means","o":["Transfer of spores as unit of life from other planets to Earth","Creation of life from dead and decaying matter","Creation of life from chemicals","Origin of sperm in human testes"],"c":0,"s":"Early Greek thinkers believed that units of life, called spores, came from other planets to Earth.\nThis is the theory of panspermia."},
{"id":"neet-2022r-q194","pn":194,"y":"Re-NEET 2022 · 4 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Mutation theory","q":"Select the correct statement regarding mutation theory of evolution.","o":["Large differences due to mutations arise gradually in a population","This theory was proposed by Alfred Wallace","Variations are small directional changes","Single step large mutation is a cause of speciation"],"c":3,"s":"Mutation theory was given by Hugo de Vries, not Wallace.\nMutations are large differences that appear suddenly.\nThey are random and directionless.\nA single-step large mutation can cause speciation."},
{"id":"neet-2022-q198","pn":198,"y":"NEET 2022 · 17 Jul","tp":"Evolution","st":"Homologous and analogous organs","q":"Which of the following statement is not true?","o":["Sweet potato and potato is an example of analogy","Homology indicates common ancestry","Flippers of penguins and dolphins are a pair of homologous organs","Analogous structures are a result of convergent evolution"],"c":2,"s":"Penguin and dolphin flippers do the same job (swimming) but differ in structure, so they are analogous, not homologous. Statement (3) is false.\nHomologous organs share structure and show common ancestry.\nAnalogous structures arise by convergent evolution.\nSweet potato is a modified root and potato a modified stem, so they are analogous."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q118","pn":118,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Founder effect","q":"The factor that leads to Founder effect in a population is :","o":["Genetic drift","Natural selection","Genetic recombination","Mutation"],"c":0,"s":"When a few individuals start a new isolated population, chance changes in allele frequency follow. This chance change is genetic drift.\nFounder effect is one form of genetic drift (the other is the bottleneck effect)."},
{"id":"neet-2021-q187","pn":187,"y":"NEET 2021 · 12 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Types of evolution","q":"Match List - I with List - II\nList-I: (a) Adaptive radiation (b) Convergent evolution (c) Divergent evolution (d) Evolution by anthropogenic action\nList-II: (i) Selection of resistant varieties due to excessive use of herbicides and pesticides (ii) Bones of forelimbs in Man and Whale (iii) Wings of Butterfly and Bird (iv) Darwin Finches","o":["(a)-(i), (b)-(iv), (c)-(iii), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)"],"c":1,"s":"Adaptive radiation from one ancestor: Darwin's finches, a-iv.\nConvergent evolution gives analogous organs: wings of butterfly and bird, b-iii.\nDivergent evolution gives homologous organs: forelimb bones of man and whale, c-ii.\nHuman-caused evolution: resistant varieties after heavy herbicide and pesticide use, d-i."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q002","pn":2,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Adaptive radiation and human impact","q":"Which of the following refer to correct example(s) of organisms which have evolved due to changes in environment brought about by anthropogenic action ? (a) Darwin’s Finches of Galapagos islands (b) Herbicide resistant weeds (c) Drug resistant eukaryotes (d) Man-created breeds of domesticated animals like dogs","o":["(a) and (c)","(b), (c) and (d)","only (d)","only (a)"],"c":1,"s":"Evolution due to human action: herbicide-resistant weeds, drug-resistant eukaryotes and man-made dog breeds.\nDarwin's finches evolved by natural causes, not by human action.\nSo (b), (c) and (d)."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q013","pn":13,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Convergent evolution","q":"Flippers of Penguins and Dolphins are examples of:","o":["Convergent evolution","Industrial melanism","Natural selection","Adaptive radiation"],"c":0,"s":"Penguin and dolphin flippers look alike and do the same job but have different origins.\nThis is convergent evolution (analogous organs)."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q032","pn":32,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Evidences for evolution","q":"Embryological support for evolution was disapproved by :","o":["Alfred Wallace","Charles Darwin","Oparin","Karl Ernst von Baer"],"c":3,"s":"Karl Ernst von Baer noted that embryos of different animals do not show the adult features of others, so he disproved the embryological support."},
{"id":"neet-2020-q072","pn":72,"y":"NEET 2020 · 13 Sep","tp":"Evolution","st":"Origin of life","q":"From his experiments, S.L. Miller produced amino acids by mixing the following in a closed flask :","o":["CH₃, H₂, NH₄ and water vapor at 800°C","CH₄, H₂, NH₃ and water vapor at 600°C","CH₃, H₂, NH₃ and water vapor at 600°C","CH₄, H₂, NH₃ and water vapor at 800°C"],"c":3,"s":"Miller used CH₄, H₂, NH₃ and water vapour at 800°C with electric discharge in a closed flask.\nThis gave amino acids."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q006","pn":6,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Human evolution","q":"Match the hominids with their correct brain size :\n(a) Homo habilis (i) 900 cc\n(b) Homo neanderthalensis (ii) 1350 cc\n(c) Homo erectus (iii) 650-800 cc\n(d) Homo sapiens (iv) 1400 cc\nSelect the correct option.","o":["(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(iii), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iv), (d)-(ii)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(ii), (c)-(i), (d)-(iv)"],"c":0,"s":"Brain sizes: Homo habilis 650-800 cc.\nHomo erectus about 900 cc.\nHomo sapiens about 1350 cc.\nNeanderthal man about 1400 cc.\nSo (a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(i), (d)-(ii)."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q053","pn":53,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Hardy-Weinberg principle","q":"A gene locus has two alleles A, a. If the frequency of dominant allele A is 0.4, then what will be the frequency of homozygous dominant, heterozygous and homozygous recessive individuals in the population?","o":["0.16(AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.36(aa)","0.16 (AA); 0.36(Aa); 0.48(aa)","0.36 (AA); 0.48 (Aa); 0.16 (aa)","0.16 (AA); 0.24 (Aa); 0.36 (aa)"],"c":0,"s":"p = 0.4, so q = 1 − 0.4 = 0.6.\nAA = p² = 0.16.\nAa = 2pq = 2 × 0.4 × 0.6 = 0.48.\naa = q² = 0.36.\nThe three add up to 1.\nNote: The printed solution gives Aa as 0.24 (it leaves out the factor 2); the correct 2pq is 0.48, which matches the printed key."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q055","pn":55,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Mutation theory","q":"Variations caused by mutation, as proposed by Hugo de Vries, are :","o":["small and directional","small and directionless","random and directional","random and directionless"],"c":3,"s":"De Vries said mutations are large, sudden and random.\nThey have no set direction.\nSo they are random and directionless."},
{"id":"neet-2019-q064","pn":64,"y":"NEET 2019 · 5 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Natural selection","q":"In a species, the weight of newborn ranges from 2 to 5 kg. 97% of the newborn with an average weight between 3 to 3.3 kg survive whereas 99% of the infants born with weights from 2 to 2.5 kg or 4.5 to 5kg die. Which type of selection process is taking place ?","o":["Disruptive Selection","Cyclical Selection","Directional Selection","Stabilizing Selection"],"c":3,"s":"Babies of average weight survive.\nBabies at both extremes die.\nSelection favours the middle value and removes both ends.\nThis is stabilizing selection."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q153","pn":153,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Homologous organs","q":"The similarity of bone structure in the forelimbs of many vertebrates is an example of","o":["Homology","Analogy","Convergent evolution","Adaptive radiation"],"c":0,"s":"Organs with the same basic structure and origin but different functions are homologous.\nVertebrate forelimbs (whale flipper, bat wing, human arm) have the same bone plan.\nSo this is homology."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q155","pn":155,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Divergent and convergent evolution","q":"Among the following sets of examples for divergent evolution, select the incorrect option :","o":["Forelimbs of man, bat and cheetah","Heart of bat, man and cheetah","Brain of bat, man and cheetah","Eye of octopus, bat and man"],"c":3,"s":"Divergent evolution produces homologous structures from a common ancestral origin.\nForelimbs, hearts and brains of mammals are homologous.\nThe eye of octopus and the eyes of mammals have different origins and are analogous (convergent evolution), so (4) is incorrect."},
{"id":"neet-2018-q173","pn":173,"y":"NEET 2018 · 6 May","tp":"Evolution","st":"Hugo de Vries","q":"According to Hugo de Vries, the mechanism of evolution is","o":["Multiple step mutations","Saltation","Phenotypic variations","Minor mutations"],"c":1,"s":"De Vries worked on evening primrose and proposed that evolution occurs by large, single-step mutations called saltations."}
];
